**Retention judgement.** Keep it. `policy_base_premium` now states the domain sentence in one line, and each summand names the charging policy it owns. The component charging policy (rate, block size, block price, and the "alike" grouping key) is now reachable through one named entry point, `component_blocks_base_premium`, which is where the next behavior ("3 runes + 3 moonstones → two separate blocks") and any future reinterpretation of "alike" will land. Rule 2 gain outranks the two added elements under Rule 4.

**Rule 4 (fewest elements) check.** Two functions added, both private, both named for a distinct MHPCO charging policy that changes independently (price-list revision vs block-offer revision). Neither is a wrapper over a single call: each holds a distinct iteration strategy that the other does not share. No element is unused; no symmetry was created for its own sake.

**Second seam considered and rejected.** `apply_policy_modifiers` currently applies only the first-insurance surcharge and ignores `_customer`. A trial to split per-modifier functions (loyalty, follow-up, first insurance) would be speculative — only one modifier exists today, and the remaining three are inactive behaviors. Splitting now would invent structure ahead of the domain evidence, so I left it as one cohesive "policy-wide modifiers" decision. Likewise `component_charge` is one cohesive decision (block price or per-unit price) with no independently varying axis inside it.

## Report

Rules reviewed: all four, in order.

**Retained change** — in `/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-10-01_12-27-53_claim-office-rust-example-mapping_exact-ptdd-v1.1-refactor-subagent-cc_opus-5-no-thinking/src/lib.rs`, the body of `policy_base_premium` was replaced and two private functions inserted immediately above it. Exact resulting text:

```rust
/// Main items are charged one price-list row each, individually.
fn main_items_base_premium(items: &[Item]) -> f64 {
    items
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|item| item.item_type.main_item_base_premium())
        .sum()
}

/// Components are charged per group of alike components, so that a group of
/// exactly 3 earns the block price.
fn component_blocks_base_premium(items: &[Item]) -> f64 {
    COMPONENT_TYPES
        .iter()
        .map(|component_type| component_charge(alike_component_count(items, *component_type)))
        .sum()
}

/// The policy base premium is the sum of the items' base premiums, with alike
/// components charged as blocks.
pub fn policy_base_premium(items: &[Item]) -> f64 {
    main_items_base_premium(items) + component_blocks_base_premium(items)
}
```

Nothing was renamed, moved, or deleted. `policy_base_premium`'s signature, visibility, and doc comment are unchanged; its old two-`let` body is gone. No other function, constant, type, or test was touched. No tests were activated or edited.

**Undone changes:** none — the single trial succeeded.

**Domain-boundary outcome:** credible seam found and made visible. Responsibility: "the policy base premium is the sum of each insured item's price-list charge, with alike components charged as blocks." Independent change axes: (1) main-item price-list rows, (2) component charging policy including block size, block price, and the meaning of "alike". The seam separates these two; the previously-rejected `COMPONENT_TYPES`/`main_item_base_premium` consolidation was not retried.

**Semantic effect:** the next behavior ("3 runes + 3 moonstones → two separate blocks") and any future reinterpretation of "alike" now have one obvious destination, `component_blocks_base_premium`, instead of an inline expression in the policy sum.

**Fewest Elements:** two private functions added, each owning a distinct, independently-varying charging policy and a distinct iteration strategy. Justified under Rule 2 over Rule 4.

**Predictions:** baseline green predicted and observed (13 passed / 35 ignored); extraction predicted behavior-preserving and warning-free beyond the known `dead_code`, observed exactly that.

**Final test result:** `cargo test --offline` — 13 passed, 0 failed, 35 ignored. `cargo clippy --offline` — only the known, expected `years_with_mhpco` dead-code warning, left in place.

